        <Options>
            <Main applyHouseholdOperatingConditions="false" applyReducedOperatingConditions="false" atypicalElectricalLoads="false" waterConservation="true" referenceHouse="false">
                <Vermiculite code="3">
                    <English>No Vermiculite</English>
                    <French>Pas de Vermiculite</French>
                </Vermiculite>
            </Main>
            <WaterConservation LowFlushToilets="2" LowFlushToiletsUpgrade="2" />
            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

During the blower door test, there were many areas that had associated air leakage from your home:



** The 6" chimney for the current oil furnace.



** The older door at the back &amp; some of the windows, especially the ones in the basement.



** The header above the basement wall that adjoins the neighbouring unit, and inside the storage room in the basement



** The wall plugs &amp; switches in exterior walls, and the attic access panel



Savings: If you proceed with a Deep Home Energy Retrofit of your house, I estimate you can achieve about a 30% reduction in air leakage. This is estimated to save $ 475 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 15.3 GJ, and qualify for a $1,000 rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP), if you complete an eligible wall insulation upgrade.



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program</airSeal>
                <mainWalls>

The exterior walls of your house are 2x4 R12 and the previous owner indicated that an additional 1.5" (R7.5) of exterior insulation was added during a previous renovation. We discussed your interest to possibly add more insulation to the exterior of these walls in conjunction with the new Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program that was launched on April 1, 2019. There are 3 tiers of rebates associated with how much insulation is added ($3,000 for adding R10 - R14, $5,000 for adding R15 - R19, and $8,000 for adding R20+). For this house, I have modeled another R10 - R11, bringing the total R-value up to the minimum R30 that is generally required for new construction in the NWT.



Savings:

By increasing 100% of the exterior walls by a minimum R10, you could potentially save $ 200 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 6.4 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 3,000 in rebates from the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP), This rebate is for increasing 100% of the exterior walls, including the wall between the main floor that adjoins the rear mud room.



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program</mainWalls>
                <ceilings />
                <exposedFloors />
                <foundation />
                <windows>

The windows in this house (all except for the basement) were last updated in 1989 (30 years ago) and were good windows at the time. Some of the hardware is failing &amp; you will do some research to see if the hardware can be replaced or perhaps it is the time to update the windows. If you proceed with an exterior wall insulation upgrade of a minimum additional R10, this would represent a good time to update the windows, as you could qualify for the new $250 per window rebate.



I have modeled the potential energy savings associated with upgrading all of the current windows with ENERGY STAR?????? qualified windows rated for climate zone 3. In addition, I recommend full frame replacement windows that are triple pane, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged windows, with a U-Value (rate of heat loss) no higher than 0.25 BTU per hour.



Savings: 

By replacing the current windows with ones that meet the criteria listed above, you could potentially save $ 278 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 9.0 GJ, and qualify for $ 250 per window (up to 12 window) in rebates through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program. 



To be eligible for a windows rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, exterior main wall insulation is required. You must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program

</windows>
                <VentilationSystem />
                <HotWaterSystem>

Your house currently has a 182 litre electric hot water tank. For you future planning, we talked about an opportunity for you to consider an upgrade to an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with an energy factor of 0.92 or higher.



Savings: By replacing your current hot water tank with an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with a thermal efficiency of 0.92 or higher, could potentially save $ 686 per year in energy costs and qualify for up to $ 1,250 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program</HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>

Your house is currently heated with an older oil furnace (estimated 83% efficiency). In conjunction with a Deep Home Energy Retrofit of your house (more insulation &amp; less air leakage) I have modeled an upgrade to an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified high-efficiency condensing propane furnace with a minimum efficiency of 95% AFUE, including a high efficiency ECM motor to circulate the heat.



Savings: By replacing your current furnace with a high-efficiency condensing propane furnace, that incorporates an ECM fan blower, with an efficiency of 95% AFUE, you could potentially save $ 609 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 27.5 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 3,000 in rebates from the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP). If you choose not to participate in the DHERP, you would still be eligible for a $1,500 rebate on this furnace through our Energy Efficiency Incentive Program (EEIP)



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms for DHERP, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program



For more information and rebate application forms for EEIP, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program</HeatingSystem>
                <Renewables />
                <WaterConservation />
                <AdditionalComments>

Ventilation



Your home currently has very little mechanical ventilation:

        - there is no kitchen range hood fan

        - there is one bathroom fan that appears to have a weak operation

        - there is a clothes dryer that exhausts to the exterior



You may want to consider installing an ENERGY STAR rated bathroom fan that seals well, uses little electricity &amp; operates at low noise levels. Some newer bathroom fans have a built-in humidity sensor. Many homes now have timer switches connected to bathroom fans (where you select 5, 10, 15, 20 minutes) so these fans can remain on for an adequate time to remove bathroom moisture and/or odours without the risk of being left on all day and wasting heat.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Depressurization Test



The result of the exhaust devices depressurization test, which consists of turning on all exhaust equipment (the bathroom fan and the dryer), indicated a pressure drop of 1.3 Pascals. As this number is less than 5 Pascals, the use of this equipment is not estimated to cause combustion products to be drawn into your home, if you have any chimney vented appliances in your home that are not direct-vent. Your current oil furnace vents up a chimney and can be susceptible to back drafting.



During my assessment of your home, there were smoke or carbon monoxide (CO) detectors present. Please ensure both types of detectors are always installed &amp; operational in your home, and not beyond their "replace by" date.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Fuel Costs - Fort Smith



The following Nov. 1, 2018 values, including GST, have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Fort Smith:

Electricity - $18.90 min, $0.25 per kWh

Oil  - $1.17 per litre 

Propane - $0.87 per litre

Wood - $242 per cord delivered; $100 per cord self-harvested

Pellets - $8.49 per bag, $288.46 per tonne bulk delivered



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Disclaimer



The foregoing report is provided as an assessment of the visible components only and shall not be taken as a guarantee or an acceptance of liability for future performance of any part of the building.  This report is prepared with reasonable skill and care within the limitations of a visual inspection on the inspection date. This evaluation and report do not cover code compliance issues set by the authority having jurisdiction.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Your Energy Advisor



Please do not hesitate to contact me if you have any questions.



Sincerely,



Ken Baigent, Energy Management Specialist

Arctic Energy Alliance

Phone: (867) 920-3333

Fax: (867) 873-0303

E-mail: Ken.Baigent@aea.nt.ca



Arctic Energy Alliance: Taking action on high heating and ation costs, and climate change!</AdditionalComments>
            </RURComments>
